  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1239 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nNarrow upper-level ridging is building into western Montana \nbehind a departing weak shortwave, tightening the surface pressure\ngradient along the Rocky Mountain Front and keeping the plains \nbreezy today while. Wind-protected valleys remain cooler under \nlingering inversions, though light winds and gradual mixing will \nkeep conditions quiet across southwest Montana through the \nremainder of the day. \n\nWesterly flow strengthens tonight through Friday as a Pacific \ntrough moves atop a ridge over the Great Basin and southwestern \nCONUS. Strong 700mb winds of 50 to 60 kts spread across the \nplains, peaking late Thursday into early Friday morning. Combined \nwith mountain wave activity, strong gusts will surface along the \nRocky Mountain Front, adjacent high plains, and Judith Basin \nCounty, while Pacific moisture brings accumulating snow to the \nContinental Divide on Thanksgiving. \n\nA secondary trough dives southeastward Friday into Saturday, \nshifting flow northwesterly. Lingering daytime mixing and wave \nactivity will keep winds breezy across the plains Friday \nafternoon, though below warning thresholds. Meanwhile, colder air \nexpands mountain snow into central and southwest Montana and drops\nsnow levels toward lower elevations by Saturday morning. Beyond \nthis weekend, models are increasingly confident in the development\nof an upper-level ridge across the southwestern US, though its \nmagnitude and general placement remain very uncertain. Depending \non how far west or east the ridge sets up and how strongly it \namplifies, Montana could end up in cooler northwest flow with \nperiodic mountain snow, breezy westerly flow with Divide-focused \nprecipitation, or milder and drier southwesterly flow. -JR\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHigh Wind Warnings have been issued to replace the High Wind \nWatches along the Rocky Mountain Front, adjacent plains, and \nJudith Basin County, running primarily from Thursday morning through\nearly Friday morning. Relying on mountain wave breaking after \nsunset Thursday, NBM probabilities for gusts exceeding 60 mph sit \naround 65% out toward Cut Bank with similar probabilities of gusts\nexceeding 75 mph along the RMF including East Glacier and \nBrowning. Warnings remain confined to the current footprint, \npersistent cloud cover across Cascade County should limit mixing \nand keep Great Falls gusts largely below 50 mph (90th percentile \naround 60 mph). Winds will remain breezy through Friday afternoon \nafter the warnings expire before quickly calming with the loss of \ndiurnal heating. Warm overnight temperatures with a crusted \nsnowpack keep blowing snow impacts minimal along the Rocky \nMountain Front. \n\nConfidence is high that Pacific moisture will bring accumulating \nsnow along the Continental Divide on Thanksgiving while \ndownsloping keeps lower elevations dry. Winter weather advisories\nwere issued for the Glacier Park and Marias Pass area to highlight\nthe concern for hazardous travel especially with the Holiday\ntravel surge.\n\nConfidence has also increased in the broader Friday into Saturday\nsystem, particularly with the primary QPF axis focusing across \nsouthwest Montana, though exact snowfall amounts and localized \nplacement remain uncertain. Snow levels dropping to 3,000 to 4,000\nfeet across southwest Montana by Saturday morning, and down to \nthe plains floor further north, will allow for lower elevation \nsnow. Any appreciable lower elevation accumulations will likely \nfavor northwest-facing foothills of the central and southwest \nmountains, while northern plains locations face much lower \nconfidence in seeing measurable precipitation despite cold enough \nthermal profiles. -JR",
